WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
Welcome to Unintended Consequences, episode 44 for June 30th, 2026.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:10.580
Today's profile, a policy that did the opposite of what its designers intended.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.180
On a Tuesday morning in February 2003, drivers approaching the newly marked boundary of central

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.400
London encountered a 5-pound daily charge they had never paid before.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.640
Picture the scene at one of the entry points along the inner ring road.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.820
A motorist who had previously driven straight through what would become the priced zone

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.460
now had to decide whether to pay the flat fee or find another route entirely.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.300
The painted red lines and camera gantries made the boundary unmistakable,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:46.000
yet nothing in the physical layout of the surrounding streets had changed to absorb the vehicles

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.340
that might choose to avoid crossing it.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.840
Inside the zone, traffic volumes fell noticeably within weeks.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.120
Transport for London's early counts showed roughly 15% fewer vehicles entering during

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:05.280
charging hours, which translated if you start with a baseline of 100 vehicles on a typical morning

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.880
to only about 85 vehicles making the same trip.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.940
That reduction eased conditions for buses and remaining cars within the eight square mile area,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:21.960
yet the same arithmetic implied that 15 vehicles per 100 had to go somewhere else or not travel at all.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.180
Outside the painted red lines however, residents in Hackney, Tower Hamlets,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.040
and parts of Islington began recording longer queues on their local high streets,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.100
and higher readings on air quality monitors that had previously shown steadier numbers.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.860
These were not the main radial routes that planners had modeled most carefully.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.180
They were the residential connectors and shopping streets that sat just beyond the cordon.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.360
Monitors that had once registered gradual improvements now captured incremental

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:59.300
rises in nitrogen dioxide at certain boundary sites, a shift that appeared smaller or even reversed

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.020
compared with the clearer drops recorded inside the zone itself.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:09.460
Ken Livingstone, elected mayor of London in 2000, had inherited a city whose central streets were

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:15.860
among the most congested in Europe. Average speeds during peak hours had fallen below 10 miles per hour,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:21.140
which meant that a journey that should have taken 15 minutes could stretch to 25 or 30,

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:27.780
and bus reliability suffered as a direct result because the same slow-moving traffic delayed every

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:33.720
service that crossed the core. Livingstone and his transported visors drew on earlier studies,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.280
including work by the London Planning Advisory Committee in the 1990s,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:44.640
that treated pricing as a practical way to manage scarce road space rather than simply adding more lanes.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.260
Those studies had shown that even modest charges could shift some trips to other times or modes,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:57.480
and the policy aligned with the new Greater London Authorities Powers under the 1999 Act,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.780
which explicitly allowed road user charging as one of the tools available to the mayor.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.600
At the time, the dominant concern was the visible gridlock inside the historic core.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.620
The outer burrows were viewed mainly as corridors feeding that core rather than as places that

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:21.160
might absorb displaced flows. One obvious objection is whether planners simply overlooked the

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:27.600
boundary roads. The record shows that some outer burrow counselors did raise displacement concerns during

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:34.900
the 2001-2002 consultation, yet the prevailing view was that any spillover effects could be studied

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:42.320
after the scheme began and adjusted if necessary. The charge took effect on February 17, 2003,

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.400
across an eight-square-mile zone bounded by the Inner Ring Road.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.200
Cameras recorded number plates at entry points, and drivers could pay by midnight the same day,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.160
removing the need for physical barriers while still creating a sharp financial line.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:04.220
Within the first month, Transport for London reported a 15% drop in vehicles entering the zone

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:10.480
during charging hours, and a 30% reduction in congestion measured by journey time delays.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.900
If 100 vehicles had previously crossed a cordon point, the new figure was roughly 85.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.240
The time-saved on each remaining trip compounded across thousands of daily movements.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.660
Livingstone described the scheme as a spectacular success in his 2004 progress report,

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:35.200
because those central numbers improved so quickly. Early monitoring focused on central counts

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.620
and bus speeds. Boundary road data were collected, but received less immediate public attention because

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:47.380
the primary goal had been to demonstrate relief inside the zone. Traffic that previously crossed

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:53.060
the zone now stopped short or diverted around it. Counts on the Merlebone Road and other

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:59.440
Inner Ring Road sections rose by roughly 4-10% in the first year, according to Transport for

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:05.480
London's own boundary monitoring. If a road had carried 1,000 vehicles on an average day before

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:14.040
the charge, the new total could reach 1,040-1,100, enough to lengthen cues at signals and increase

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.760
idling. In residential streets, just north and east of the zone, local authorities recorded

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:26.000
increases in through traffic that had not been modeled in detail beforehand. Delivery vans and

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.760
smaller commercial vehicles, facing the flat daily fee, sometimes consolidated fewer trips or

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.600
altered routes in ways that concentrated activity on perimeter roads rather than spreading it evenly.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.840
Residents in Hackney and Islington reported longer waits at local junctions and in some cases

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.980
rerouted school runs that added minutes to already tight schedules. The causal chain was straight

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:56.520
forward once the numbers are examined step by step. A cordon price creates a sharp discontinuity

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:03.640
at its edge. Inside the line, the cost is 5 pounds, outside it is zero. Drivers rationally seek

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:09.700
the lowest cost path that avoids the priced area, which means any trip whose origin and destination

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:15.060
can be connected without crossing the cordon will shift to the unpriced boundary roads.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.780
Because the scheme did not price or restrict the boundary roads themselves,

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:25.780
those roads became the new equilibrium points for marginal trips that could no longer use the

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:32.280
interior as a shortcut. An obvious objection is that many drivers might simply cancel trips or

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:38.580
switch to transit. While some did, the monitoring data showed that enough marginal traffic remained on

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:45.080
the perimeter to produce measurable increases. Second order effects included pressure on parking in

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.820
adjacent neighborhoods and complaints from bus operators whose cross boundary services encountered new

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:57.360
delays. Third order effects appeared in local planning debates as several burrows began considering

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:03.560
their own mini-charging proposals to protect residential streets from the diverted flows. Transport

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:11.400
for London expanded the zone westward in 2007 then reversed that expansion in 2011 after a change in

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:19.400
mayoral administration. The daily charge rose in steps reaching 15 pounds by 2020 and an ultra low

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:27.080
emission zone overlay was added in 2019 that extended beyond the original boundary. Continuous

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:32.480
monitoring eventually produced annual reports that tracked both central improvements and boundary

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:38.700
conditions leading to targeted junction upgrades and bus lane extensions on some perimeter routes.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:45.260
No wholesale reversal occurred. The core scheme remained in place and was later cited in the design

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:51.560
of clean air zones in other British cities. The lasting adjustment has been a broader recognition that

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:57.380
cordon systems require explicit attention to the ring of streets immediately outside the priced area.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:04.140
Any boundary drawn for pricing or regulation will shift activity to the spaces just beyond it unless

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:10.680
those spaces are also managed. Systems that treat the edge as neutral ground reliably discover that

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:16.360
the edge is where the next bottle neck forms. When designing interventions that rely on sharp

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:22.300
geographic distinctions, planners can usefully ask what new flows will be created at the perimeter

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:28.580
and whether those flows can be priced, calmed, or absorbed without simply relocating the original

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:34.640
problem. How might today's proposals for low traffic neighborhoods or workplace parking levies

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:39.760
account for the same boundary dynamics? That's unintended consequences for today.

